insert into kairo.questions (id, subject, topic, subtopic, learning_objective, concepts_tested, difficulty_rating, cognitive_level, estimated_solving_time_sec, reading_load, calculation_load, distractors, source, year, exam_body, stem, options, correct_option, explanation, lifecycle_state, distractor_rationale) values
($q$chemistry_0461$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Metals & Their Compounds$q$, $q$Thermal Decomposition of Metal Nitrates$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2006 question on Thermal Decomposition of Metal Nitrates.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "ndx7qp", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$application$q$, 57, $q$medium$q$, $q$light$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "Calcium is a reactive metal, so its nitrate only breaks down as far as the oxide (CaO) plus NO2 and O2 - not all the way to free calcium metal.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "B", "explanation": "Copper sits in the 'middle' reactivity band, so its nitrate also stops at the metal oxide (CuO), not the free metal - it's easy to lump this with silver if you're not tracking reactivity position carefully.", "misconceptionId": "overgeneralized_rule"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "Sodium is very reactive, so its nitrate barely decomposes at all under normal heating - it just loses oxygen to form NaNO2, without releasing NO2 or forming any free metal.", "misconceptionId": "missing_prerequisite"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2006, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$On heating, which of the following compounds will decompose to the free metal, nitrogen(IV) oxide and oxygen?$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "Ca(NO3)2", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "Cu(NO3)2", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "AgNO3", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "D", "text": "NaNO3", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$C$q$, $q$The trick here is knowing that not all nitrates decompose the same way when heated - it depends on where the metal sits on the reactivity series. Very reactive metals (Na, K) only lose oxygen, giving the nitrite. Moderately reactive metals (Mg up to Cu) break down to the metal oxide plus NO2 and O2. But metals low on the reactivity series - like silver - are so unstable as compounds that heat pushes all the way down to the free metal itself. That's why 2AgNO3 -> 2Ag + 2NO2 + O2.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: Calcium is a reactive metal, so its nitrate only breaks down as far as the oxide (CaO) plus NO2 and O2 - not all the way to free calcium metal. B: Copper sits in the 'middle' reactivity band, so its nitrate also stops at the metal oxide (CuO), not the free metal - it's easy to lump this with silver if you're not tracking reactivity position carefully. D: Sodium is very reactive, so its nitrate barely decomposes at all under normal heating - it just loses oxygen to form NaNO2, without releasing NO2 or forming any free metal.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0462$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Metals & Their Compounds$q$, $q$General Reactivity Facts$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2006 question on General Reactivity Facts.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "vfmsto", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$recall$q$, 25, $q$low$q$, $q$none$q$, $q$[{"option": "B", "explanation": "Malleability and ductility describe how a metal behaves under stress - they say nothing about whether it reacts with its surroundings.", "misconceptionId": "misunderstood_terminology"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "This is actually backwards - alkali metals have unusually LOW melting and boiling points for metals, and this property has nothing to do with why they're not found free anyway.", "misconceptionId": "memorized_not_understood"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "Conducting heat and electricity is a general metallic property shared by nearly all metals, reactive or not - it doesn't explain occurrence in nature.", "misconceptionId": "misunderstood_terminology"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2006, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$The group IA metals are not found free in nature because they$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "are very reactive and unstable", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "B", "text": "are malleable and ductile", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "have high melting and boiling point", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "conduct heat and electricity", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$A$q$, $q$An element only exists free (uncombined) in nature if it's unreactive enough to survive on its own - think gold, or the noble gases. Alkali metals are the opposite: they react violently with water, and even just moisture or oxygen in the air, so they instantly form compounds. That's why you'll never dig up a lump of pure sodium.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$B: Malleability and ductility describe how a metal behaves under stress - they say nothing about whether it reacts with its surroundings. C: This is actually backwards - alkali metals have unusually LOW melting and boiling points for metals, and this property has nothing to do with why they're not found free anyway. D: Conducting heat and electricity is a general metallic property shared by nearly all metals, reactive or not - it doesn't explain occurrence in nature.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0463$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Nature of Matter$q$, $q$Allotropy$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2006 question on Allotropy.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "1e6r0v", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$application$q$, 37, $q$low$q$, $q$none$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "Graphite is one of carbon's pure elemental allotropes - just carbon atoms bonded to each other, not combined with a different element.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "B", "explanation": "Coal is usually grouped with the free forms of carbon (it's classed alongside graphite and diamond as impure elemental carbon), which trips students up since it doesn't look 'pure'.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "Diamond is the purest elemental allotrope of carbon - the exact opposite of a combined form.", "misconceptionId": "memorized_not_understood"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2006, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$Carbon exists naturally in the combined state as$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "graphite", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "coal", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "diamond", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "wood", "isCorrect": true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$D$q$, $q$Don't rush - 'combined' has a specific meaning here: carbon chemically bonded to other elements, forming a compound. Graphite, diamond, and coal are all classed as forms of free (elemental) carbon - just pure carbon atoms arranged differently, or carbon with impurities. Wood is different: it's mostly cellulose, where carbon is genuinely bonded to hydrogen and oxygen in a real compound. That makes wood the true example of carbon in the combined state.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: Graphite is one of carbon's pure elemental allotropes - just carbon atoms bonded to each other, not combined with a different element. B: Coal is usually grouped with the free forms of carbon (it's classed alongside graphite and diamond as impure elemental carbon), which trips students up since it doesn't look 'pure'. C: Diamond is the purest elemental allotrope of carbon - the exact opposite of a combined form.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0464$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Rates of Reaction$q$, $q$Activation Energy and Catalysts$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2006 question on Activation Energy and Catalysts.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "c4kxyo", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$recall$q$, 25, $q$low$q$, $q$none$q$, $q$[{"option": "B", "explanation": "Solid catalysts in heterogeneous catalysis usually don't dissolve at all - they work right on their surface, so solubility isn't the relevant factor.", "misconceptionId": "misunderstood_terminology"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "Concentration is a term for dissolved species in solution; a solid catalyst isn't 'concentrated' in that sense.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "Just having more mass of catalyst doesn't help if it's shaped as one big block - a small amount broken into powder can outperform a bigger lump because more surface is exposed.", "misconceptionId": "overgeneralized_rule"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2006, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$The efficiency of a solid catalyst to increase reaction rates depends on its$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "surface area", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "B", "text": "solubility", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "concentration", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "quantity", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$A$q$, $q$A solid catalyst speeds up a reaction by giving reactant molecules a surface to land on and react at (active sites). The more surface exposed, the more of these active sites are available at once - which is exactly why catalysts are often used as fine powders, pellets, or mesh rather than solid lumps.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$B: Solid catalysts in heterogeneous catalysis usually don't dissolve at all - they work right on their surface, so solubility isn't the relevant factor. C: Concentration is a term for dissolved species in solution; a solid catalyst isn't 'concentrated' in that sense. D: Just having more mass of catalyst doesn't help if it's shaped as one big block - a small amount broken into powder can outperform a bigger lump because more surface is exposed.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0465$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Chemical Equilibrium$q$, $q$Dynamic equilibrium concept$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2006 question on Dynamic equilibrium concept.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "kggaw3", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$recall$q$, 25, $q$low$q$, $q$none$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "Simply being reversible doesn't make something an equilibrium - equilibrium is specifically about the rates of the forward and reverse steps matching.", "misconceptionId": "misunderstood_terminology"}, {"option": "B", "explanation": "This is a classic trap - equilibrium is about equal RATES, not equal concentrations. In most real equilibria, reactant and product concentrations are quite different from each other.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "This describes a one-way, irreversible reaction going to completion - the exact opposite of the back-and-forth balance that defines equilibrium.", "misconceptionId": "overgeneralized_rule"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2006, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$Dynamic equilibrium describes a situation where$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "a reaction gives back the reactant", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "the concentration of the reactant and products is equal", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "both forward and reverse reactions proceed at the same rate", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "D", "text": "reactants are converted to products", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$C$q$, $q$The word 'dynamic' is the giveaway - the reaction hasn't stopped, both directions are still happening, just at matching speeds. So concentrations stay constant not because nothing's moving, but because whatever forms is being used up again just as fast.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: Simply being reversible doesn't make something an equilibrium - equilibrium is specifically about the rates of the forward and reverse steps matching. B: This is a classic trap - equilibrium is about equal RATES, not equal concentrations. In most real equilibria, reactant and product concentrations are quite different from each other. D: This describes a one-way, irreversible reaction going to completion - the exact opposite of the back-and-forth balance that defines equilibrium.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0466$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Metals & Their Compounds$q$, $q$Amphoteric Metals$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2006 question on Amphoteric Metals.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "q8of3z", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$application$q$, 45, $q$low$q$, $q$light$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "Ca(OH)2 is only sparingly soluble to begin with, so it doesn't give a clean, distinct white precipitate the way Zn(OH)2 does, and it certainly doesn't redissolve in excess base.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "B", "explanation": "Mg(OH)2 does give a genuine white precipitate, but it's not amphoteric - it stays put even when you add excess NaOH.", "misconceptionId": "overgeneralized_rule"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "Cu2+ fails at the first step - it gives a pale blue precipitate, not white, so it's ruled out before you even test solubility in excess.", "misconceptionId": "misunderstood_terminology"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2006, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$The ion that will give a white precipitate with aqueous NaOH and is soluble in excess of the base is$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "Ca2+", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "Mg2+", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "Zn2+", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "D", "text": "Cu2+", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$C$q$, $q$This is where students usually get caught mixing up two separate tests: the colour of the precipitate, and whether it redissolves in excess. Zn(OH)2 forms white and is amphoteric - it dissolves back into excess NaOH to form a soluble zincate ion. That combination (white AND redissolves) is what makes zinc unique among these four.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: Ca(OH)2 is only sparingly soluble to begin with, so it doesn't give a clean, distinct white precipitate the way Zn(OH)2 does, and it certainly doesn't redissolve in excess base. B: Mg(OH)2 does give a genuine white precipitate, but it's not amphoteric - it stays put even when you add excess NaOH. D: Cu2+ fails at the first step - it gives a pale blue precipitate, not white, so it's ruled out before you even test solubility in excess.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0467$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Metals & Their Compounds$q$, $q$Flame Tests$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2006 question on Flame Tests.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "865p59", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$recall$q$, 25, $q$low$q$, $q$none$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "Lilac is potassium's flame colour, not copper's - an easy mix-up if you haven't drilled the list.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "Yellow belongs to sodium - and sodium's flame is so strong it can even mask other colours if a sample isn't pure.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "Brick-red is calcium's signature flame colour, not copper's.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2006, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$The characteristic colour of the flame shown when a copper salt moistened with concentrated hydrochloric acid on a platinum wire is put on a Bunsen burner is$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "lilac", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "green", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "C", "text": "yellow", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "brick-red", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$B$q$, $q$This is pure recall, so it's worth locking the flame colours in as a set: copper gives green (sometimes described as blue-green), potassium gives lilac, sodium gives yellow, and calcium gives brick-red.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: Lilac is potassium's flame colour, not copper's - an easy mix-up if you haven't drilled the list. C: Yellow belongs to sodium - and sodium's flame is so strong it can even mask other colours if a sample isn't pure. D: Brick-red is calcium's signature flame colour, not copper's.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0468$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Chemistry & Industry$q$, $q$Syngas$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2006 question on Syngas.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "kt2ylw", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$application$q$, 45, $q$low$q$, $q$light$q$, $q$[{"option": "B", "explanation": "Phosphorus(V) oxide is a drying agent - it removes water vapour, not carbon dioxide, so it solves the wrong problem entirely.", "misconceptionId": "misunderstood_terminology"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "Lime water is the standard laboratory test for confirming CO2 is present - but it's not practical or economical as an industrial-scale removal method.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "Ammonical copper(I) chloride is used to absorb carbon(II) oxide (CO) in gas analysis - it's for a different gas altogether.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2006, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$In the industrial production of H2 from natural gas, the CO2 produced along with the H2 is removed by$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "washing under pressure", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "B", "text": "drying over phosphorus(V) oxide", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "passing the mixture into lime water", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "using ammonical copper(I) chloride", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$A$q$, $q$This is where students usually mix up a lab TEST with an industrial PROCESS. Lime water genuinely does react with CO2 - but that's a small-scale identification test, not something you can run continuously on a huge industrial gas stream. On a plant scale, CO2 is scrubbed out economically by washing the gas mixture under pressure. Same target gas, very different scale of solution.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$B: Phosphorus(V) oxide is a drying agent - it removes water vapour, not carbon dioxide, so it solves the wrong problem entirely. C: Lime water is the standard laboratory test for confirming CO2 is present - but it's not practical or economical as an industrial-scale removal method. D: Ammonical copper(I) chloride is used to absorb carbon(II) oxide (CO) in gas analysis - it's for a different gas altogether.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0469$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Non-Metals & Their Compounds$q$, $q$Sulphur Compounds$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2006 question on Sulphur Compounds.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "2eamtt", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$application$q$, 45, $q$low$q$, $q$light$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "FeS dissolves easily in dilute HCl, releasing H2S gas - it's actually the standard method for generating H2S in the lab.", "misconceptionId": "overgeneralized_rule"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "ZnS also dissolves readily in dilute HCl, just like FeS - both behave very differently from CuS.", "misconceptionId": "overgeneralized_rule"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "Na2S is a Group 1 sulphide, already fully soluble in water itself before you even add acid - the question is really about the less reactive metals.", "misconceptionId": "missing_prerequisite"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2006, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$The sulphide which is insoluble in dilute hydrochloric acid is$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "FeS", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "CuS", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "C", "text": "ZnS", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "Na2S", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$B$q$, $q$Don't assume all metal sulphides behave the same way with acid - it depends on how tightly the metal holds onto the sulphide ion. FeS and ZnS dissolve readily in dilute HCl (this is actually how H2S gas is made in the lab). But CuS has such a low solubility, its structure resists dilute acid entirely - you'd need concentrated nitric acid to break it down.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: FeS dissolves easily in dilute HCl, releasing H2S gas - it's actually the standard method for generating H2S in the lab. C: ZnS also dissolves readily in dilute HCl, just like FeS - both behave very differently from CuS. D: Na2S is a Group 1 sulphide, already fully soluble in water itself before you even add acid - the question is really about the less reactive metals.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0470$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Non-Metals & Their Compounds$q$, $q$Chlorine Water and Photodecomposition$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2006 question on Chlorine Water and Photodecomposition.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "gbh920", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$application$q$, 45, $q$low$q$, $q$light$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "Hydrogen and oxygen combining to form water is triggered by a spark or flame - it's driven by heat/ignition energy, not light specifically.", "misconceptionId": "misunderstood_terminology"}, {"option": "B", "explanation": "The N2O4/NO2 interconversion is a temperature-dependent equilibrium, shifting with heat rather than light.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "Calcium hydroxide breaking down to calcium oxide is a straightforward thermal decomposition - it needs heat, not light, to proceed.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2006, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$Which of the following typically represents a photochemical reaction?$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "Formation of water from hydrogen and oxygen molecules", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "Conversion of dinitrogen(IV) oxide to nitrogen(IV) oxide", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "Decomposition of calcium hydroxide to its oxide", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "Conversion of silver halides to grey metallic silver", "isCorrect": true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$D$q$, $q$A photochemical reaction is specifically one driven by light energy, not heat or a spark. The classic textbook example is photographic film: silver halide crystals (like AgBr) turn into grey metallic silver the moment light hits them - that's literally how old-style photography captured an image.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: Hydrogen and oxygen combining to form water is triggered by a spark or flame - it's driven by heat/ignition energy, not light specifically. B: The N2O4/NO2 interconversion is a temperature-dependent equilibrium, shifting with heat rather than light. C: Calcium hydroxide breaking down to calcium oxide is a straightforward thermal decomposition - it needs heat, not light, to proceed.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0471$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Chemistry & Industry$q$, $q$Natural Gas Composition$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2006 question on Natural Gas Composition.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "xlxlql", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$recall$q$, 25, $q$low$q$, $q$none$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "Ethene is usually produced by cracking larger hydrocarbons in refineries, not by biological decay of organic matter.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "CO2 is more characteristic of aerobic decomposition, where oxygen IS available - the question specifically asks about the anaerobic (oxygen-free) pathway.", "misconceptionId": "overgeneralized_rule"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "Nitrogen(IV) oxide has no real connection to organic decay - it's an easy pick if you're guessing rather than reasoning through the biology.", "misconceptionId": "guessed_no_reasoning"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2006, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$The gas obtained as a product of anaerobic action on organic matter buried in the earth is$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "ethene", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "methane", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "C", "text": "carbon(IV) oxide", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "nitrogen(IV) oxide", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$B$q$, $q$Anaerobic just means 'without oxygen' - and when bacteria break down buried organic matter with no oxygen around, the gas they release is methane (sometimes called 'marsh gas' or biogas). This is the same process, over geological time, that builds up natural gas deposits underground.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: Ethene is usually produced by cracking larger hydrocarbons in refineries, not by biological decay of organic matter. C: CO2 is more characteristic of aerobic decomposition, where oxygen IS available - the question specifically asks about the anaerobic (oxygen-free) pathway. D: Nitrogen(IV) oxide has no real connection to organic decay - it's an easy pick if you're guessing rather than reasoning through the biology.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0472$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Chemistry & Industry$q$, $q$Petroleum Refining$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2006 question on Petroleum Refining.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "yxuq7s", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$application$q$, 37, $q$low$q$, $q$none$q$, $q$[{"option": "B", "explanation": "Natural gas content isn't what the light/heavy crude classification is describing - that classification is about the density and composition of the liquid oil itself.", "misconceptionId": "misunderstood_terminology"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "Octane number is a property measured for refined petrol, not for the raw, unrefined crude oil coming out of the ground.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "This is backwards - light crude oils are actually rich in aliphatic (paraffinic) hydrocarbons, not low in them.", "misconceptionId": "overgeneralized_rule"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2006, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$Nigerian crude oil is described as light crude because of its$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "low sulphur content", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "B", "text": "high natural gas content", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "high octane number", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "low aliphatic-hydrocarbon content", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$A$q$, $q$Nigerian crude is famous on the world market as 'light, sweet' crude - light referring to its lower density, sweet referring to its low sulphur content. These two traits go hand in hand, which is why the exam key ties the 'light' description to low sulphur: it means the oil needs less processing to strip out impurities, making it more valuable to refiners.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$B: Natural gas content isn't what the light/heavy crude classification is describing - that classification is about the density and composition of the liquid oil itself. C: Octane number is a property measured for refined petrol, not for the raw, unrefined crude oil coming out of the ground. D: This is backwards - light crude oils are actually rich in aliphatic (paraffinic) hydrocarbons, not low in them.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0473$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Organic Chemistry$q$, $q$Hydrocarbons — Homologous Series$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2006 question on Hydrocarbons — Homologous Series.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "r4t0dz", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$recall$q$, 25, $q$low$q$, $q$none$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "Pentane is aliphatic, but it's fully saturated - all single bonds, no double bonds anywhere in the chain.", "misconceptionId": "misunderstood_terminology"}, {"option": "B", "explanation": "Butanol is an aliphatic alcohol, but it's saturated - the -OH group doesn't create unsaturation.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "Benzene is unsaturated, but it's aromatic (a ring structure), which is the opposite classification from aliphatic.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2006, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$An example of an aliphatic unsaturated hydrocarbon is$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "pentane", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "butanol", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "propene", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "D", "text": "benzene", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$C$q$, $q$You need both boxes ticked here: aliphatic means an open chain (not a ring), and unsaturated means it has a C=C or C≡C bond. Propene is a straight-chain alkene - it satisfies both conditions at once.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: Pentane is aliphatic, but it's fully saturated - all single bonds, no double bonds anywhere in the chain. B: Butanol is an aliphatic alcohol, but it's saturated - the -OH group doesn't create unsaturation. D: Benzene is unsaturated, but it's aromatic (a ring structure), which is the opposite classification from aliphatic.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0474$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Chemistry & Industry$q$, $q$Soap and Detergents — Structure and Function$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2006 question on Soap and Detergents — Structure and Function.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "gqm7dj", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$application$q$, 37, $q$low$q$, $q$none$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "Palm wine mixes completely with the aqueous detergent solution - there's no immiscible phase left to form droplets from.", "misconceptionId": "misunderstood_terminology"}, {"option": "B", "explanation": "Ethanoic acid is fully miscible with water in all proportions, so it just dissolves rather than forming an emulsion.", "misconceptionId": "misunderstood_terminology"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "Detergent plus water alone is simply a solution - you need a second, immiscible liquid present to emulsify in the first place.", "misconceptionId": "missing_prerequisite"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2006, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$An emulsion may be produced by shaking a detergent solution with$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "palm wine", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "ethanoic acid", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "palm oil", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "D", "text": "water", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$C$q$, $q$An emulsion needs two liquids that don't normally mix, with a third substance (here, the detergent) stabilizing tiny droplets of one dispersed through the other. Palm oil and water don't mix on their own - shaking them with detergent creates that fine dispersion. Palm wine, ethanoic acid, and water are all miscible with water already, so there's no separate phase to disperse in the first place.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: Palm wine mixes completely with the aqueous detergent solution - there's no immiscible phase left to form droplets from. B: Ethanoic acid is fully miscible with water in all proportions, so it just dissolves rather than forming an emulsion. D: Detergent plus water alone is simply a solution - you need a second, immiscible liquid present to emulsify in the first place.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0475$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Chemical Combination$q$, $q$Combustion Calculations$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2006 question on Combustion Calculations.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "l5d6y3", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$recall$q$, 25, $q$low$q$, $q$none$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "Hydrogen is a component that's already locked up in the wax molecule itself - it can't also show up unreacted as a product of burning.", "misconceptionId": "misunderstood_terminology"}, {"option": "B", "explanation": "Oxygen is consumed as a reactant during combustion - it can't simultaneously be a leftover product.", "misconceptionId": "misunderstood_terminology"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "Carbon(II) oxide (CO) only forms under incomplete combustion, when oxygen supply is limited - the default assumption for a candle burning normally is complete combustion, giving CO2.", "misconceptionId": "missing_prerequisite"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2006, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$The products of the combustion of candle wax are$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "hydrogen and water", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "oxygen and water", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "carbon(IV) oxide and water", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "D", "text": "carbon(II) oxide and water", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$C$q$, $q$Candle wax is a hydrocarbon, so the default rule applies: complete combustion of any hydrocarbon in plenty of oxygen gives carbon(IV) oxide and water as the only products.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: Hydrogen is a component that's already locked up in the wax molecule itself - it can't also show up unreacted as a product of burning. B: Oxygen is consumed as a reactant during combustion - it can't simultaneously be a leftover product. D: Carbon(II) oxide (CO) only forms under incomplete combustion, when oxygen supply is limited - the default assumption for a candle burning normally is complete combustion, giving CO2.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0476$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Atomic Structure & Bonding$q$, $q$Periodic Trends$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2006 question on Periodic Trends.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "2oceg6", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$application$q$, 45, $q$medium$q$, $q$light$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "This flips the actual direction - the tighter grip from higher nuclear charge makes losing an electron harder, not easier, across a period.", "misconceptionId": "sign_error"}, {"option": "B", "explanation": "Reactivity doesn't rise in one simple direction across a period - metals become less reactive while non-metals become more reactive toward gaining electrons, so a blanket 'increase' oversimplifies the trend.", "misconceptionId": "overgeneralized_rule"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "This is backwards too - as elements become less electropositive across a period, covalent bonding tendency generally increases rather than decreases.", "misconceptionId": "sign_error"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2006, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$A decrease in atomic size and an increase in nuclear charge across a period lead to$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "an increase in the tendency to lose electron", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "an increase in chemical reactivity", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "a decrease in electropositivity", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "D", "text": "a decrease in covalency in bond formation", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$C$q$, $q$Picture what's happening physically: as you move across a period, more protons pull the same outer shell in tighter, so electrons are held more firmly. That makes it harder, not easier, to lose an electron - which is exactly what a decrease in electropositivity means (moving away from 'metal-like' behaviour toward non-metal behaviour).$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: This flips the actual direction - the tighter grip from higher nuclear charge makes losing an electron harder, not easier, across a period. B: Reactivity doesn't rise in one simple direction across a period - metals become less reactive while non-metals become more reactive toward gaining electrons, so a blanket 'increase' oversimplifies the trend. D: This is backwards too - as elements become less electropositive across a period, covalent bonding tendency generally increases rather than decreases.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0477$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Separation of Mixtures$q$, $q$Separation Techniques$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2006 question on Separation Techniques.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "kc1jua", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$application$q$, 57, $q$low$q$, $q$light$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "Dissolving first, before removing the sublimable ammonium chloride, means it just dissolves too, leaving no way to separate it cleanly from the sodium chloride afterward.", "misconceptionId": "rushed_under_pressure"}, {"option": "B", "explanation": "Filtration only makes sense once you have a solid-liquid mixture; trying it on the dry solids first accomplishes nothing since all three components start out solid.", "misconceptionId": "missing_prerequisite"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "Sublimation has to happen on the dry mixture before anything gets wet - once you've dissolved the mixture in water, ammonium chloride is now in solution and can no longer be sublimed out separately.", "misconceptionId": "missing_prerequisite"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2006, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$The order in which a mixture of sand, ammonium chloride and sodium chloride can be separated is$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "dissolution → dryness → filtration → evaporation", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "filtration → sublimation → evaporation → dryness", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "sublimation → dissolution → filtration → evaporation", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "D", "text": "dissolution → filtration → sublimation → evaporation", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$C$q$, $q$Walk through it one property at a time. Ammonium chloride is the odd one out because it sublimes on heating - so heat the dry mixture first and let it escape, leaving sand and sodium chloride behind. Then dissolve that residue in water: sodium chloride dissolves, sand doesn't. Filter to pull out the insoluble sand. Finally, evaporate the filtrate to recover solid sodium chloride. Each step targets exactly one property, in the order it becomes useful: sublimation → dissolution → filtration → evaporation.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: Dissolving first, before removing the sublimable ammonium chloride, means it just dissolves too, leaving no way to separate it cleanly from the sodium chloride afterward. B: Filtration only makes sense once you have a solid-liquid mixture; trying it on the dry solids first accomplishes nothing since all three components start out solid. D: Sublimation has to happen on the dry mixture before anything gets wet - once you've dissolved the mixture in water, ammonium chloride is now in solution and can no longer be sublimed out separately.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0478$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Atomic Structure & Bonding$q$, $q$History of Atomic Theory$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2006 question on History of Atomic Theory.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "nucy3t", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$recall$q$, 37, $q$low$q$, $q$none$q$, $q$[{"option": "B", "explanation": "Thomson's 'plum pudding' model pictured electrons scattered through a diffuse positive sphere - there was no distinct nucleus or orbiting motion in his idea at all.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "Bohr is easy to mix up with Rutherford here since his diagrams look similar, but Bohr's real contribution was adding quantized orbits/energy levels on top of Rutherford's original planetary idea.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "Dalton's atom was a solid, indivisible sphere with no internal structure at all - no nucleus, no orbiting electrons.", "misconceptionId": "missing_prerequisite"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2006, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$The idea of planetary electron was that of$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "Rutherford", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "B", "text": "Thompson", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "Bohr", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "Dalton", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$A$q$, $q$This is where students often get caught, because Bohr's diagrams look similar. But the 'planetary model' - electrons circling a tiny, dense nucleus the way planets orbit the sun - comes from Rutherford's 1911 gold-foil scattering experiment. Bohr came afterward and refined it by adding fixed, quantized energy levels; that refinement is Bohr's specific contribution, but the original planetary picture belongs to Rutherford.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$B: Thomson's 'plum pudding' model pictured electrons scattered through a diffuse positive sphere - there was no distinct nucleus or orbiting motion in his idea at all. C: Bohr is easy to mix up with Rutherford here since his diagrams look similar, but Bohr's real contribution was adding quantized orbits/energy levels on top of Rutherford's original planetary idea. D: Dalton's atom was a solid, indivisible sphere with no internal structure at all - no nucleus, no orbiting electrons.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0479$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Acids, Bases & Salts$q$, $q$Titration Calculations$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2006 question on Titration Calculations.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "e6pk77", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$application$q$, 72, $q$low$q$, $q$heavy$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "This magnitude shows up if you forget to convert cm3 to dm3 properly somewhere in the calculation, inflating the answer by orders of magnitude.", "misconceptionId": "unit_conversion_error"}, {"option": "B", "explanation": "This comes from adjusting the volumes correctly but forgetting to divide by 2 for the mole ratio between HCl and Na2CO3, landing one decimal place too high.", "misconceptionId": "arithmetic_slip"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "This turns up when the ratio is set up backwards - dividing the wrong quantities against each other instead of tracking moles through step by step.", "misconceptionId": "formula_recall_error"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2006, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$Na2CO3(aq) + 2HCl(aq) -> 2NaCl(aq) + H2O(l) + CO2(g). What is the concentration of a sodium trioxocarbonate(IV) solution, 25 cm3 of which is exactly neutralized by 10 cm3 of 0.05 mol dm-3 hydrochloric acid solution?$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "1.000 mol dm-3", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "0.100 mol dm-3", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "0.010 mol dm-3", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "D", "text": "0.111 mol dm-3", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$C$q$, $q$Before touching a calculator, notice this is really a mole-ratio problem in disguise. Moles of HCl used = 0.010 dm3 x 0.05 mol dm-3 = 0.0005 mol. The equation shows 2 mol HCl reacts with every 1 mol Na2CO3, so moles of Na2CO3 = 0.0005 / 2 = 0.00025 mol. That's the amount present in the 25 cm3 (0.025 dm3) sample, so concentration = 0.00025 / 0.025 = 0.010 mol dm-3.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: This magnitude shows up if you forget to convert cm3 to dm3 properly somewhere in the calculation, inflating the answer by orders of magnitude. B: This comes from adjusting the volumes correctly but forgetting to divide by 2 for the mole ratio between HCl and Na2CO3, landing one decimal place too high. D: This turns up when the ratio is set up backwards - dividing the wrong quantities against each other instead of tracking moles through step by step.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0480$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Energy Changes$q$, $q$Comparing Enthalpy Changes$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2006 question on Comparing Enthalpy Changes.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "6i2n9s", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$application$q$, 57, $q$low$q$, $q$light$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "This comes from doubling the value (as if writing 2H2 + O2 -> 2H2O) and also flipping the sign - two errors stacked on the underlying formula for how the equation scales.", "misconceptionId": "formula_recall_error"}, {"option": "B", "explanation": "This keeps the correct sign but doubles the magnitude, mixing up the per-mole value with the doubled equation where ΔH would be -571.6 kJ for 2 mol of water.", "misconceptionId": "formula_recall_error"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "This has the right size but the wrong sign - it forgets that forming a stable compound like water from its elements releases heat rather than absorbing it.", "misconceptionId": "sign_error"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2006, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$If the heat of combustion of hydrogen is -285.8 kJ, what is the heat of formation of water?$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "+571.6 kJ", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "-571.6 kJ", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "-285.8 kJ", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "D", "text": "+285.8 kJ", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$C$q$, $q$Don't rush into recalculating anything here - notice that this is a conceptual question dressed up as a numbers question. Burning hydrogen (H2 + 1/2 O2 -> H2O) IS, by definition, the same reaction as forming water from its elements. So the heat of combustion of hydrogen and the heat of formation of water are literally the same value for the same equation: -285.8 kJ. The negative sign makes sense too, since forming a stable compound like water releases energy.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: This comes from doubling the value (as if writing 2H2 + O2 -> 2H2O) and also flipping the sign - two errors stacked on the underlying formula for how the equation scales. B: This keeps the correct sign but doubles the magnitude, mixing up the per-mole value with the doubled equation where ΔH would be -571.6 kJ for 2 mol of water. D: This has the right size but the wrong sign - it forgets that forming a stable compound like water from its elements releases heat rather than absorbing it.$q$);
